/** @page overview-vinecop Vine copula models

@tableofcontents

- [How to read the R-vine structure](#vinecop-matrix)
- [Set up a custom vine copula model](#vinecop-custom)
- [Fit and select a vine copula model](#vinecop-fit)
- [Work with a vine copula model](#vinecop-work)
- [Inspecting the trees](#vinecop-trees)
- [Serialization](#vinecop-serialization)

Vine copula models are implemented in the class `Vinecop`. To use this class in
your code, include the header `vinecopulib/vinecop/class.hpp` (or simply
`vinecopulib.hpp`) at the top of your source file. This automatically enables
all features for bivariate copula models.

Conditional simulation is covered in @ref conditional, discrete variables in
@ref discrete, and the score, gradient and Hessian of the log-likelihood in
@ref inference.

@section vinecop-matrix How to read the R-vine structure

The structure is held by `RVineStructure`, which stores it as a triangular
array: rows are trees, and each column encodes one edge per tree. For

```
4 4 4 4
3 3 3
2 2
1
```

the pair copulas are

| tree | edge | pair copula  |
|------|------|--------------|
| 0    | 0    | (1, 4)       |
|      | 1    | (2, 4)       |
|      | 2    | (3, 4)       |
| 1    | 0    | (1, 3; 4)    |
|      | 1    | (2, 3; 4)    |
| 2    | 0    | (1, 2; 3, 4) |

Writing `M[i, j]` for the entry in row `i` and column `j`, the edge `e` of tree
`t` in a `d`-dimensional vine is
`(M[d - 1 - e, e], M[t, e]; M[t - 1, e], ..., M[0, e])`: start at the
counter-diagonal element of the column for the first conditioned variable, jump
up to row `t` for the second, and collect everything above it as the
conditioning set.

`RVineStructure` documents the full list of validity conditions, the
natural-order normalization, and how truncation is represented.

@note The representation changed in release 1.0.0: `get_matrix()`,
`get_order()`, `get_struct_array()` and the orientation of individual pair
copulas differ from earlier releases for the same model. Densities and
log-likelihoods are unaffected. See @ref migrating-to-1-0.

`RVineStructure` has two order-determined specializations, `CVineStructure` and
`DVineStructure`, and can also generate random structures.

@snippet vinecop.cpp structure

@section vinecop-custom Set up a custom vine copula model

A model is a structure plus a `std::vector<std::vector<Bicop>>` of pair copulas,
shaped like the structure's triangular array. `Vinecop::make_pair_copula_store()`
builds one of the right shape, filled with independence copulas.

As for bivariate copulas, there are two ways of setting up a vine:
- with known parameters,
- from data (i.e., with estimated parameters).

The constructors without data take a dimension (giving a D-vine of independence
copulas), or a structure (or an R-vine matrix) together with the pair copulas.

@snippet vinecop.cpp custom

@section vinecop-fit Fit and select a vine copula model

`select()` performs parameter estimation and model selection using the sequential
procedure of
[Dissmann et al. (2013)](https://mediatum.ub.tum.de/doc/1079277/1079277.pdf). It
selects the structure as well as the families, unless the object already carries
a structure, in which case that structure is kept. `fit()` re-estimates families
and parameters on a fixed structure. The only mandatory argument is the data; the
controls argument customizes the fit.

@snippet vinecop.cpp fit

`FitControlsVinecop` inherits every member of `FitControlsBicop` (see
@ref bicop-fit) and adds the structure knobs:

- `size_t trunc_lvl` — the number of trees that are actually fit. Pair copulas in
  trees **above** this level are set to independence. Defaults to no truncation.
- `std::string tree_criterion` — the edge-weighting criterion used to grow each
  tree: `"tau"` (default) for Kendall's tau, `"rho"` for Spearman's rho,
  `"hoeffd"` for Hoeffding's D (suited to non-monotonic relationships), `"mcor"`
  for the maximum correlation, `"joe"` for the Gaussian-copula mutual
  information, or `"custom"` for a user-supplied callable set through
  `set_tree_criterion_function()`, which is always evaluated on the calling
  thread.
- `double threshold` — pair copulas whose weight falls below this are set to
  independence (default `0`, i.e. no thresholding).
- `std::string tree_algorithm` — how the spanning tree is chosen:
  `"mst_prim"` (default) or `"mst_kruskal"` for maximum spanning trees, or
  `"random_weighted"` / `"random_unweighted"` for random ones.
- `bool select_trunc_lvl` — select the truncation level automatically by mBICv
  (default `false`).
- `bool select_threshold` — select the threshold automatically (default `false`).
- `bool select_families` — whether families are selected at all, as opposed to
  keeping those already in place (default `true`).
- `std::vector<size_t> conditioning_set` — variables to place at the tail of the
  order, so they can be conditioned on later (see @ref conditional).
- `bool show_trace` — print progress during fitting.
- `std::vector<int> seeds` — seeds for the random tree algorithms.

@snippet vinecop.cpp controls

@section vinecop-work Work with a vine copula model

You can simulate from a model and evaluate its density, distribution function
(by Monte Carlo), log-likelihood and information criteria, apply the Rosenblatt
transform and its inverse, and truncate an existing model.

`pdf_full()` returns, alongside the density, the per-edge pair-copula densities
and h-functions computed on the way; the derivative routines reuse them rather
than recomputing the cascade.

@snippet vinecop.cpp model

@section vinecop-trees Inspecting the trees

`get_trees()` returns an `RVineTrees`: the structure as an explicit edge list,
tree by tree. Each edge carries its conditioned pair `(a, b)`, its conditioning
set `C`, and its pair copula. `RVineStructure` can be constructed back from an
`RVineTrees`, which makes this a round trip and a convenient way to build a
structure edge by edge.

@snippet vinecop.cpp trees

@section vinecop-serialization Serialization

As for bivariate copulas, models can be written to and constructed from JSON or
CBOR files and [`nlohmann::json` objects](https://github.com/nlohmann/json). A
`.cbor` extension selects the binary encoding; any other extension is JSON.

@snippet vinecop.cpp serialization

*/
